{
  "FormatVersion": 3,
  "Id": "cd5a182e-254b-4e65-820b-ff754122614c"/*VideoDeviceInput*/,
  "Description": "Captures live video from a connected device like a webcam or capture card, with the basic camera hardware configs built in: Pan, Tilt, Zoom, Focus, Exposure and Brightness are applied to the camera automatically whenever their input changes — several settings at once, and each can be patched/animated.\n\nBeyond the fixed config inputs, a generic control surface reaches every control the device actually exposes — the replacement for the old DirectShow capture-filter dialog: `ControlName` offers the discovered controls as a dropdown, `ControlValue` carries the value in device units (for menu controls it picks one of the item values its dropdown lists), `ControlAuto` is a strict two-way switch (true = engage the control's auto mode, value ignored; false = apply the value as manual, which disengages auto), and `ApplyControl` sends the combination to the camera. The `ControlsCatalog` output lists everything the device reports — kind, range, step, default, current value and auto/inactive state. On platforms without a camera-control backend, both report controls as unavailable.\n\nDevices are opened through FFmpeg — DirectShow on Windows, V4L2 on Linux, AVFoundation on macOS. You can select the device, resolution, and frame rate; the capture runs on its own thread and automatically retries when a device is unplugged and returns. Basic image manipulation like flipping, rotation, scaling and repositioning is done in software.\n\nTips:\n- To save system resources, enable `DeactivateWhenNotShowing`. This will pause the capture when the operator is not being rendered.\n- The config inputs only touch the camera when their value changes, and only if the camera actually implements the control. Values are in device units (Zoom 100 = 1x, Pan/Tilt in 1/64°); `Status` shows the supported range after an apply. All control traffic runs on a background worker, so success notes, clamping and unsupported warnings land one evaluation after the change, and an animated input coalesces into one pending write.\n- The resolution and frame-rate dropdowns list the combinations the device reports (on macOS the device doesn't expose them, so only defaults are offered); with `ResolutionFpsType` set to `Custom`, any value can be typed — the driver negotiates the closest match and the image is resized in software when the device can't deliver the requested size.\n- On macOS the camera is gated by privacy: if the editor is denied camera access, no devices appear — allow it under System Settings → Privacy & Security → Camera, then trigger `Reconnect`.\n- A `Reconnect` trigger also rescans the device list, so cameras plugged in after startup become selectable, and refreshes the control catalog of the (possibly replaced) camera.\n- The `Status` output provides useful information for troubleshooting connection issues.\n\nAKA: webcam, camera, capture card, ptz, pan, tilt, zoom, video input",
  "InputUis": [
    {
      "InputId": "11f7432d-31f1-44b9-8f75-b1569b314b13"/*CustomFps*/,
      "Position": {
        "X": -200.0,
        "Y": 375.0
      },
      "Description": "Manually specify a frame rate. This will only work if the device and its driver support the requested FPS. Default is 0 (device default)."
    },
    {
      "InputId": "22513b82-e77a-417a-8a46-24e677f072d4"/*DeactivateWhenNotShowing*/,
      "Position": {
        "X": -200.0,
        "Y": 150.0
      },
      "Description": "If enabled, the device is automatically deactivated when its output is not being rendered, which can save significant system resources."
    },
    {
      "InputId": "236d4c5c-0022-4416-a22c-d6df73c306e2"/*Active*/,
      "Position": {
        "X": -200.0,
        "Y": 0.0
      },
      "Description": "Enables or disables the video capture."
    },
    {
      "InputId": "3022de8a-5d88-4a37-9799-780f2a838a5f"/*FlipVertically*/,
      "Position": {
        "X": -200.0,
        "Y": 450.0
      },
      "Description": "Flips the incoming video image vertically."
    },
    {
      "InputId": "3022de8a-5d88-4a37-9799-780f2a838a6e"/*ApplyRotationData*/,
      "Position": {
        "X": -200.0,
        "Y": 600.0
      },
      "Description": "Applies a rotation to the video image in degrees. This is used for correcting orientation."
    },
    {
      "InputId": "49019d29-873e-4b7c-a897-c575a384a650"/*ResolutionFpsType*/,
      "Position": {
        "X": -200.0,
